        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            New asphalt is walkable after about 24 hours and drivable after about 2 to 3 days, longer in hot weather, but it takes roughly 6 to 12 months to fully cure as its lighter oils evaporate and the binder hardens. Until then, and especially on hot days in the first summer, it is soft enough to dent and scuff under point loads and twisting tires.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protect it with a few habits: straight in and straight out, no turning the wheel while stopped, plywood under kickstands and jacks, no heavy trucks or parking on the edges, and fuel and oil kept off the surface. Then wait 6 to 12 months before the first sealcoat. If parts of the driveway stay soft, rut or hold water, call your contractor while the warranty is still in force.
          </p>
